This question already has an answer here
:
Why does a Java Lambda which throws a Runtime Exception require brackets?
(1 answer)
Closed
2 years ago
.
If there is a single statement in a lambda function, we can omit defining the full code block for it:
new Thread(() -> System.out.println());
Why is that not the case for statements that throw exceptions? This yields a compilation error stating
'{' expected
:
new Thread(() -> throw new RuntimeException());
Of course, enclosing the lambda body in a code block works:
new Thread(() -> {
    throw new RuntimeException();
});

